[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Data Migration and Schema Evolution Patterns in Firebase Firestore"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike traditional relational databases with rigid schemas, Firebase Firestore is a NoSQL document database. While this schema-less nature provides immense flexibility during early development, it introduces unique challenges when your data model needs to evolve over time. This tutorial explores production-ready patterns for executing data migrations and handling schema evolution smoothly in Firestore."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Schema-less Challenge"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Firestore, documents within the same collection can have completely different fields and data types. When your application logic changes (such as renaming a field, splitting a string into an object, or changing a data type), existing documents in the database remain untouched. If your code expects the new schema without falling back gracefully, it can lead to runtime exceptions or application crashes."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Pattern 1: Lazy Migration (On-the-Fly Upgrades)"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Lazy Migration pattern updates documents incrementally as users interact with them. When a document is read, your application checks if it conforms to the current schema version. If it is outdated, the application transforms the data in memory, writes the updated document back to Firestore, and then proceeds."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"async function getUserProfile(userId) {
  const docRef = db.collection("users").doc(userId);
  const docSnap = await docRef.get();

  if (!docSnap.exists) return null;

  let data = docSnap.data();

  // Check if migration is needed (e.g., schema version < 2)
  if (!data.schemaVersion || data.schemaVersion < 2) {
    data = migrateUserToV2(data);
    // Persist the migrated data back to Firestore
    await docRef.set(data, { merge: true });
  }

  return data;
}"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: No expensive batch operations required; zero downtime; migrates only active data."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: Inactive or legacy documents remain unmigrated indefinitely; adds minor latency to the first read."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Pattern 2: Eager Migration via Background Workers"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"For critical schema changes where all documents must be updated immediately (such as security rules enforcing new required fields), you must use an eager migration strategy. This involves writing a dedicated background script, often using Node.js with the Firebase Admin SDK, to iterate through collections and update documents in batches."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"async function migrateCollectionInBatches(batchSize) {
  const collectionRef = db.collection("users");
  let query = collectionRef.orderBy("__name__").limit(batchSize);

  let snapshot = await query.get();
  while (!snapshot.empty) {
    const batch = db.batch();

    snapshot.docs.forEach((doc) => {
      const data = doc.data();
      if (data.schemaVersion < 2) {
        const updatedData = migrateUserToV2(data);
        batch.set(doc.ref, updatedData, { merge: true });
      }
    });

    await batch.commit();

    // Get the next batch starting after the last document
    const lastDoc = snapshot.docs[snapshot.docs.length - 1];
    snapshot = await query.startAfter(lastDoc).get();
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pros: Ensures 100% database consistency; cleans up legacy structures entirely."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cons: High write volume can consume quota quickly; requires careful pagination to avoid memory overflows."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Firestore Schema Evolution"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always include a schemaVersion field in your documents to explicitly track structural iterations."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Design your application code to be defensive. Always use optional chaining or fallback values when reading fields that might belong to an older schema."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Combine patterns: Use eager background scripts for major version upgrades, and lazy migration as a fallback for edge cases."}]
